# "good (a gain)" rests on different grounds in the two cases: restful absence of disturbance in C1, the company of the dead in P4. Both still mean that death is good for the one who dies, so the sense does not change.
# One could worry that a dreamless night is "better and more pleasant" only in hindsight, and that an unconscious state is not experienced as pleasant. But P2 and A1 use the phrase the same way, so this is not a shift between statements.
